# Backend & tenancy

Applies to server and API code. `OPERATOR.md`'s "anything that runs against a database" bans
also apply; this rule adds the request-time discipline.

- **Every caller-controlled identifier is untrusted.** Body, params, query, and headers included.
  Scope every query by `req.organizationId` (derived from the JWT, never from the request), and
  validate that every related entity a write touches belongs to the same tenant before using it.
- **Preserve the tenancy guarantees.** Keep composite tenant constraints (`REFERENCES parent(id,
  organization_id)`) and the existing tenancy audits (`server/scripts/audit-tenancy.mjs`,
  `scripts/tenant-fk-audit.mjs`) green. Do not weaken a constraint to make a write pass.
- **Keep writes closed and accountable.** Preserve closed update allowlists (never spread
  arbitrary request fields into a write), authorization gates, last-admin protection, and audit
  logging on every mutation. Never `catch {}` a user-facing failure.
- **Money is exact.** Handle amounts in integer minor units, keep refunds excluded from
  aggregates (see `scripts/money-invariants.mjs`), and keep multi-step writes inside one
  transaction with clear boundaries.
- **Sacramental writes stay ceremonial.** Nothing writes `ochr_sacramental_records` except a
  human commit through the drafts sandbox. Never add a bypass.
- **Exposed surfaces need review.** Public, kiosk, token, webhook, cron, import, and export
  paths bypass the normal JWT tenant scoping, so treat any change to them as security-sensitive
  and call it out explicitly.
- **Prove isolation.** A tenant-sensitive change is not done until a test shows same-tenant
  success **and** cross-tenant rejection.
